84,"watchRatio":0.48450000000000004},{"ratio":0.85,"watchRatio":0.4817},{"ratio":0.86,"watchRatio":0.48},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.47450000000000003},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.4693},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.4661},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.4646},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.4585},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.4531},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.44799999999999995},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.4457},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.4391},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.4322},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.42369999999999997},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.4114},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.396},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.3908}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":145,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"This 17th century Brahmin minister would be branded a Hindu nationalist today, but what he actually said is far more interesting. In 1600s Golconda, a Muslim-ruled sultanate, two Brahmin ministers, Akkanna and Madanna, became its most powerful ministers. They challenged Persian court elites, strengthened local administration, and supported [music] temples and indigenous institutions. Then Akkanna told a Telugu-speaking Dutch merchant something that sounds oddly modern. \"We are devoted to this country. We have no other homeland. Foreign Muslims only want to grow rich and then leave for a land they call their fatherland.\" Sounds anti-Muslim, right? Here's the twist. Akkanna wasn't against Muslims. He allied alongside local Deccan Muslims. His enemy wasn't a faith. It was foreign elite that drained India's wealth and left its people starving. The real dividing line was the people rooted in this land versus those who came only to loot."}}